---
import screenshot from "../../../../docs/images/evabar-liquid-glass.png";
import ResourceArticle from "@/layouts/ResourceArticle.astro";
import { releasesUrl, repositoryUrl } from "@/lib/release";
import { resources } from "@/lib/resources";
---
<ResourceArticle page={resources.floating}>
  <h2>Open the calculator with a keyboard shortcut</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="/#download">Download EvaBar</a> for your Mac, unzip it, and move the app to Applications. It requires macOS 13 or later.</li>
    <li>In Applications, open <strong>Eva|Bar</strong>. If macOS blocks the beta, <a href="#open-beta">follow the first-launch steps below</a>.</li>
    <li>Switch to the app you’re working in and press the default shortcut, <kbd>Control–Shift–Space</kbd>, to bring up the calculator. You can also click the EvaBar menu bar icon when it’s visible.</li>
    <li>Type <code>(480 + 96) / 3</code>. The result updates as you type. Press <kbd>Return</kbd> to copy <strong>192</strong> and paste it where you need it.</li>
  </ol>

  <figure class="resource-figure">
    <img src={screenshot.src} width={screenshot.width} height={screenshot.height} alt="EvaBar’s floating calculator in the Liquid Glass appearance" loading="lazy" />
  </figure>

  <h2>Keep the calculator above other windows</h2>
  <p>If you have more values to calculate, click the lock before returning to your spreadsheet, editor, or browser. The calculator stays visible while you paste results and work through the remaining expressions.</p>
  <p>When you’re done, unlock the panel so a click outside it dismisses it. You can also press <kbd>Esc</kbd> while the calculator has focus. If Preferences or the function panel is open, <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes it first. With those panels closed, <kbd>Esc</kbd> hides the calculator.</p>

  <h2>Change the shortcut or open the app at login</h2>
  <p>To use a different keyboard shortcut, open <strong>… → Preferences</strong> and start recording under <strong>Global shortcut</strong>. Press a combination that includes <kbd>Control</kbd>, <kbd>Option</kbd>, or <kbd>Command</kbd>. If the app reports that the shortcut is unavailable, try another combination.</p>
  <p>While you’re in Preferences, you can also turn on <strong>Launch at login</strong> to have EvaBar ready when you sign in.</p>

  <section aria-labelledby="open-beta">
    <h2 id="open-beta">Open the EvaBar beta for the first time</h2>
    <p>The beta downloads from <a href={releasesUrl}>EvaBar’s GitHub Releases</a> are ad hoc signed. They lack an Apple Developer ID signature and notarization, so macOS may block the first launch.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the app from Applications once and dismiss the alert.</li>
      <li>Open <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong> and scroll to <strong>Security</strong>.</li>
      <li>If macOS offers <strong>Open Anyway</strong> for your copy of EvaBar from GitHub Releases, select it, then confirm and authenticate if prompted.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If macOS says <strong>“Eva|Bar is damaged and can’t be opened”</strong>, download a fresh ZIP from GitHub Releases and extract it again. Try opening the fresh copy, then use <strong>Open Anyway</strong> if macOS lists the app under <strong>Privacy &amp; Security</strong>.</p>
    <p>If the fresh copy still won’t open, <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/issues`}>report the issue</a> with your macOS version and Mac processor.</p>
    <p class="resource-source"><a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102445">Apple’s guidance on opening apps on a Mac</a>.</p>
  </section>
</ResourceArticle>
